    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i've been looking with a magnifying glass but i'm just not seeing anything. it's so weird because sometimes i'll find a cluster of holes in one spot, but then the next day, there's a new cluster a few feet away in a totally different bay, and it's like they're just jumping around. \n\nit's almost like they're avoiding the light or something. i've tried leaving a light on in the greenhouse overnight to see if i can catch them in the act, but when i come in the next morning, all the holes are there and it's still completely quiet. it's super frustrating. is there any kind of pest that specifically avoids light or is just really, really good at staying hidden even when things are bright?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A pest that moves around erratically and seems to avoid light, even when you're trying to catch them with a bright light, sounds like a very elusive creature. It's less likely to be a typical chewing insect, and more in the realm of something that moves very quickly or is extremely small, like certain types of fungus gnats' larvae or even very small, nocturnal aphids. You might be dealing with a colony that is incredibly dispersed, scattering their feeding spots across the whole greenhouse.\n\nIf they are avoiding light, you might need to change your detection strategy; instead of relying on visual spotting, try setting up pheromone traps or sticky traps in different locations throughout the bays. Sometimes, the most secretive pests are attracted to specific chemical signals, which is much more reliable than waiting for them to wander into a bright light. This constant, scattered damage is a hallmark of a population that is thriving but remaining hidden from your direct observation.",
      "source": "target",
